MOT 9/24/2013 - Modified Genplot read to be handle -APPEND option with 
   the "read -list c1 1 /" mode.  Reading into each of the specified arrays
   are treated independently, starting at the existing end of the array.
   Arrays will not be expanded, but will truncate the specific column read
   when the array is completely filled.  Other arrays will continue to fill
   as long as there is data.  Option works for both read and string arrays.
      alloc s1 string_array 100
      read text.dat -list s1 1 / 
      read text.dat -list s1 2 / -append

MOT 9/21/2013 - Added the -STRICT option to sorting strings and arrays
      GENPLOT:  alloc s1 string_array 10
      GENPLOT:  let s1[0] = "This"  let s1[1] = "That"  let s1[2] = "More"
      GENPLOT:  let s1[3] = "Less"  let s1[4] = "Any"   let s1[5] = "This"
      GENPLOT:  let s1[6] = "Who"   let s1[7] = "More"  let s1[8] = "Any"
      GENPLOT:  let s1[9] = "Hello there good looking"
      GENPLOT: sort s1 -strict
      GENPLOT: eval s1
        [00] Any
        [01] Hello there good looking
        [02] Less
        [03] More
        [04] That
        [05] This
        [06] Who
      GENPLOT:

MOT 9/21/2013 - Modified behavior of TRANSFORM CROSSING function.
   In previous implementation, the + or - nature of a crossing on the Y
   array did not consider the X values.  So if X was decreasing in the
   array, the + crossing would refer to a negative slope on the graph of
   X versus Y.  This has been changed so that the + crossing means a +
   slope of the local X versus Y curve.  If two points have the same X
   coordinate, a positive crossing is defined when y[i+1]>y[i].

  A new option was added 
      transform crossing 0.5 -POS -NOSORT
   to implement the previous behavior.  With -nosort, the X array
   is not considered an a crossing is positive if y[i+1]>y[i].  With 

MOT 8/21/2013 - Added key to turn off logarithmic tertiary labels
     AXCTRL <axis> -NOTERTitary
  Disables labeling.  -DX2 still sets the number of tertiary tick marks.
  This only eliminates the small numbers.

MOT 8/7/2013 - Added ability to change color of axes (just one)
     AXCTRL <axis> -COLOR <n>
  Will cause the specified axis to be drawn in another color.  The settings
  of $AXCOLR[] will override these settings, so can force the title to be
  in black if desired (or tick marks)
     axctrl left -color 2
     let $axcolr[4] = 1
     axis
  Note that all four axes are independent with respect to color.  Although
  the right normally follows the left characteristics (unless on), this will
  not happen with color.

MOT 8/1/2013 - New smoothing capability
  It isn't often that I'm surprised by GENPLOT anymore.  But smoothing of
  square waves turned out to look really ugly.  Learned that the FFT_SMOOTH
  is really a convolution with an inverted bell rather than with a true
  Gaussian.  So had to implement the Gaussian smooth to deal with curves
  containing very high spatial frequency "signals".  Expanded SMOOTH with
  options to handle, as well as new commands
     SMOOTH -FFT <pts>      - Smooth by the inverted Bell over <pts> width
     SMOOTH -GAussian <pts> - Smooth by true Gaussian over <pts> width
     GAUSS_SMOOTH <pts>     - Smooth by true Gaussian over <pts> width
     SMOOTH_GAUSSIAN <pts>  - Smooth by true Gaussian over <pts> width
     FFT_SMOOTH <pts>       - Existing function using inverted bell
     SMOOTH_FFT <pts>       - Existing function using inverted bell
  The effect of <pts> is the same in both cases, reducing Gaussian noise
  by a factor of approximate 1/sqrt(pts).

MOT 7/10/2013 - Statistics on probability distribution functions
   The new functions
       @pdf_ave(cv [,xl,xh])       @pdf_avg(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_average(cv [,xl,xh])   @pdf_mean(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_median(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_rms(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_std(cv [,xl,xh])       @pdf_sdev(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_stdev(cv [,xl,xh])     @pdf_stddev(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_variance(cv [,xl,xh])  @pdf_var(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_skew(cv [,xl,xh])      @pdf_skewness(cv [,xl,xh])
       @pdf_kurt(cv [,xl,xh])      @pdf_kurtosis(cv [,xl,xh])
   treat a curve as a probability distribution histogram.  The moments
   of this pdf (as an integral) are computed.  If the optional range
   is specified, the curve is assumed to be sorted in X.

MOT 7/10/2013 - Documentation of behavior
   The statistical commands @mean, @skew, etc. allow a curve as well as
   an array as the argument.  If a curve is given, the Y argument is
   treated as the array to scan.  The optional range arguments, which
   are integer indices into the array, become X values for the array.
      eval @mean($plot, -9.5, 9.5)
   returns the average value of the default Y array but only including
   points where -9.5<x<9.5. The range is optional and defaults to the
   entire domain.

MOT 6/16/2013 - Added ability to "uncurl" an AFM image
    The AFM images often come with a complex "curl" in X, Y or both,
    where curl is a "techical" term for a smoothly varying background
    that is not removed by the flatten command.  In either X or Y, the
    average height is fit to a polynomial and then subtracted from the
    data.  Some day, "uncrumple" may be added to remove interactions
    between X and Y, but currently only uncurl.
       matrix s1 uncurl x 6
    uncurls the surface s1 along the X direction using a 6th order
    polynomial fit.

MOT 4/11/2013 - Added conditions to properly read a new format of DI
    AFM format files.  From the CNF cleanroom.

MOT 3/14/2013 - Added more functions - Fermi-Dirac integrals of useful
    orders.
      fdm0p5(x) - F_{-0.5}(x)
      fdp0p5(x) - F_{0.5}(x)
      fdp1p5(x) - F_{1.5}(x)
      fdp2p5(x) - F_{2.5}(x)
    From ACM bulletin - accurate to at least 6 decimals

MOT 2/25/2013 - Modified most of the @max type functions to allow
    operation on either arrays or curves.  If the argument is an
    array, the two optional arguments are interpreted as an index
    range to limit the data (inclusive).  If the argument is a
    2D curve, the two optional arguments are interpreted as X
    values to constrain the data.
        @max(c1,-0.5,0.5)
    is now equivalent to
        retrieve c1 
        cull keep xrange -0.5 0.5
        eval @max(y)
    Makes life much nicer when scanning for baseline levels in
    complex spectra.

MOT 1/4/2013 - Large file support.  Increasingly dealing with files
    more than 2 GB in size.  This is the 2^31 limit in non-64 bit
    architectures.  For interim, have fixed the "ll" command and the
    "filesize()" functions to properly handle large files (64 bit
    limit).  This should also fix some of the date problems that will
    start cropping up about 2037 (just about the time I get to start
    thinking about retiring).

MOT 1/3/2013 - Fixed the ^C handling in much of the code.  In both 
    XGenplot and Genplot, ^C now terminates command line, file, and
    block level repeat operations.  Attempts to flush input buffer
    so that the prompt for terminating running blocks is clean.

    Modified several commands/routines to limit potential delays:
        sleep / read / plot / let (long array evaluations)

MOT 1/2/2013 - Added visibility to the SysDebugFlag internal variable
    as the function variable $SysDebugFlag.  This is a bit-wise parameter
    that can be coded to provide detailed debug information during run.
    Generally not useful to common user ... but good for programmer.
        0x02 -> In gvcalc(), prints the pseudo-op codes from calculations
        0x04 -> In gvcalc(), also prints the stack and arg values

MOT 1/1/2013 - Fixed problem with wait timeout.

MOT 12/26/2012 - Added all the ctype.h functions for determining the type
    of character.  
          isalnum(c), isalpha(c), iscntrl(c), isdigit(c), isgraph(c), 
          islower(c), isprint(c), ispunct(c), isspace(c), isupper(c),
          isxdigit(c)
    and the two to convert single characters upper and lower case
          tolower(c)
          toupper(c)
    where c is any integer value (converted to nearest integer).
    Return value is system dependent, but non-zero if value is 
